F222 (new) — an overload set with default arguments dispatches to the wrong overload

Found while investigating F221; it is a separate defect and does not cause F221.

pickHelperOverload ranked candidates by `params.length !== callArguments.length` and returned -1
otherwise. An overload whose trailing parameters carry defaults is therefore non-viable for any call
that does not supply all of them, so for such a call *every* candidate scored -1 — and the loop kept
its seed, the first-declared overload, then emitted a call to it with the wrong arity. Extra
arguments were dropped silently.

C++ viability: an overload with P parameters of which D carry defaults accepts P-D through P
arguments. This carries the declared default onto the compiled parameter, makes viability the
[minArgs, maxArgs] range, prefers an exact arity match between two viable candidates, seeds the
search with a viable candidate, and supplies the default at the call site.

Measured, with a probe of the shape qpi.h's DateAndTime::add has (a three-parameter overload beside
a six-required/two-defaulted one): the six-argument call answered 600 (the three-parameter body)
against clang's 615, and now answers 615. Suite 1085/0.

